  1| \chapter{The First Law of Thermodynamics}\label{ch:b1:first-law}
  2| 
  3| Pump up a bicycle tire and the barrel of the pump gets too hot to hold.
  4| A diesel engine has no spark plug: it squeezes its air twenty times
  5| smaller, and the air becomes hot enough to light the fuel by itself. A
  6| cylinder of compressed gas frosts over as it empties. In each case
  7| energy changes form --- work becomes heat, heat becomes work, both
  8| become internal energy --- and the first law of thermodynamics is the
  9| bookkeeping that makes the accounts balance. This chapter defines work
 10| and heat for a thermodynamic system, states the first law, introduces
 11| internal energy and enthalpy as the quantities it conserves, and applies
 12| it to the transformations of a perfect gas and to calorimetry.
 13| 
 14| \begin{omfigure}
 15| \includegraphics[width=0.36\linewidth]{images/book3/ai/b3-22-bicycle-pump.jpg}
 16| 
 17| {\small A bicycle pump: the work of the hand on the trapped air raises its internal energy, and the barrel gets warm --- the first law in the palm.}
 18| \end{omfigure}
