 20| \section{Transformations, work, and heat}
 21| 
 22| \begin{definition}[System, transformation]\label{def:b1:first-law:transformation}
 23| \index{thermodynamic system}\index{transformation}\index{quasi-static transformation}\index{reversible transformation}
 24| A \emph{thermodynamic system} is the matter inside a chosen boundary;
 25| everything else is the \emph{surroundings}. A \emph{transformation} takes
 26| it from an equilibrium state to another. It is \emph{quasi-static} if the
 27| system passes through a succession of equilibrium states (slow enough
 28| for $P$, $T$ to be defined throughout), and \emph{reversible} if, in
 29| addition, reversing the external conditions reverses the path (no
 30| friction, no finite temperature or pressure gap with the surroundings).
 31| Names by what stays constant: \emph{isothermal} ($T$), \emph{isobaric}
 32| ($P$), \emph{isochoric} ($V$); \emph{adiabatic} if no heat is exchanged;
 33| \emph{monobaric} (\emph{monothermal}) if the external pressure
 34| (temperature) is constant, whatever happens inside.
 35| \end{definition}
 36| 
 37| \begin{proposition}[Work of pressure forces]\label{prop:b1:first-law:work}
 38| \index{work!of pressure forces}
 39| A system whose volume changes by $\dd V$ against an external pressure
 40| $P_{\mathrm{ext}}$ receives the work
 41| \[
 42| \delta W = -P_{\mathrm{ext}}\,\dd V, \qquad
 43| W = -\int_{V_1}^{V_2}P_{\mathrm{ext}}\,\dd V .
 44| \]
 45| For a quasi-static transformation $P_{\mathrm{ext}} = P$ (the system's own
 46| pressure) and $W = -\int P\,\dd V$ is minus the area under the path in the
 47| Clapeyron diagram: positive when compressed, negative when expanding.
 48| \end{proposition}
 49| 
 50| \begin{proof}
 51| A piston of area $S$ pushed by the surroundings with the force
 52| $P_{\mathrm{ext}}S$ moves by $\dd x$ outward: the surroundings do the work
 53| $-P_{\mathrm{ext}}S\,\dd x = -P_{\mathrm{ext}}\,\dd V$ on the system. Any
 54| boundary is a collection of such pistons. Quasi-static: the piston is in
 55| equilibrium, $P_{\mathrm{ext}} = P$ up to a vanishing difference.
 56| \end{proof}
 57| 
 58| \begin{definition}[Heat]\label{def:b1:first-law:heat}
 59| \index{heat}\index{thermostat}
 60| \emph{Heat} $Q$ is energy transferred to the system other than by
 61| macroscopic work --- by molecular collisions at a wall (conduction),
 62| by the circulation of a fluid (convection), by radiation. A
 63| \emph{thermostat} is a body so large that its temperature does not change
 64| whatever heat it exchanges. Sign convention throughout: $W$ and $Q$ are
 65| \emph{received} by the system, positive when they enter.
 66| \end{definition}
 67| 
 68| \section{The first law}
 69| 
 70| \begin{theorem}[First law of thermodynamics]\label{thm:b1:first-law:firstlaw}
 71| \index{first law of thermodynamics}\index{internal energy}
 72| A closed system possesses a state function, its \emph{internal energy}
 73| $U$ (extensive), such that for any transformation
 74| \[
 75| \Delta U + \Delta E_{k} = W + Q ,
 76| \]
 77| $E_k$ being the macroscopic kinetic energy (zero for a system at rest):
 78| the energy received as work and heat is stored as internal energy. $W$
 79| and $Q$ each depend on the path; their sum does not.
 80| \end{theorem}
 81| \admitted
 82| 
 83| \begin{remark}[What it says]\label{rem:b1:first-law:meaning}
 84| It is the conservation of energy, with heat recognized as a transfer of
 85| energy (Joule's paddle-wheel experiment: a measured work always raises
 86| the temperature of water as much as a measured heat). ``$U$ is a state
 87| function'' means $\Delta U$ is fixed by the initial and final states ---
 88| so a cycle has $\Delta U = 0$ and $W + Q = 0$: an engine that delivers
 89| work must receive heat. The microscopic content of $U$ is that of
 90| \cref{def:b1:kinetic-theory:U}.
 91| \end{remark}
 92| 
 93| \begin{definition}[Enthalpy; heat capacities]\label{def:b1:first-law:enthalpy}
 94| \index{enthalpy}\index{heat capacity at constant pressure}
 95| The \emph{enthalpy} $H = U + PV$ is a state function adapted to
 96| transformations at constant pressure. The \emph{heat capacities at
 97| constant volume and pressure} are
 98| \[
 99| C_V = \left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial T}\right)_V, \qquad
100| C_P = \left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial T}\right)_P ,
101| \]
102| in \unit{J/K}; per mole $C_{V,m}$, $C_{P,m}$; per kilogram the specific
103| heats $c_V$, $c_P$.
104| \end{definition}
105| 
106| \begin{proposition}[Heat at constant volume and at constant pressure]\label{prop:b1:first-law:qvqp}
107| For a system at rest exchanging work only through pressure forces:
108| \begin{itemize}
109|   \item isochoric transformation: $W = 0$ and $Q_V = \Delta U$;
110|   \item monobaric transformation between two states at the external
111|         pressure $P_0$: $Q_P = \Delta H$.
112| \end{itemize}
113| \end{proposition}
